description:
error.rs:346-372 — ClientError::RateLimited { retry_after: jmap_types::UTCDate }

The doc-comment is honest:
"Note (bd:JMAP-6lsm.3): this base crate does not currently produce this variant. HTTP 429 responses fall through reqwest's error_for_status() and surface as ClientError::Http instead."

This is a defensive-code-forcing API shape. Every caller who wants to handle rate limiting has to write:

  match err {
    ClientError::RateLimited { retry_after } => /* handle */,
    ClientError::Http(http) if http.status() == Some(429) => {
        // ALSO handle here, because the base crate doesn't produce RateLimited
        // Parse Retry-After ourselves? Or just sleep a hardcoded duration?
    },
    _ => /* other */,
  }

The Result type lies to the caller: "if you match RateLimited, you handle rate-limiting." In reality, a caller who matches ONLY on RateLimited misses every actual 429 from the base crate.

The fix path is documented (JMAP-6lsm.3 will add 429→RateLimited conversion). But until then, every caller is exposed. The variant should arguably have been gated behind a `#[doc(hidden)]` or marked as `#[deprecated(note = "Not currently produced; match on Http with status() == 429 instead")]` to surface the situation at compile time.

Alternative recommendation: rename the doc-comment to a `# WARNING` block that's more prominent, with a code example showing the correct dual-match pattern for today. The current rustdoc is buried after the variant's #[error] string and easy to miss.

Lower priority because it's documented, and because the right fix is to implement the 429→RateLimited conversion (which is filed as a separate bead).
